{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310682,"nonce":"4+6lOL/4JQoH4+w60LiVtU4seo4=","title":"Running log of RD (recursive-descent) parser bugs in rayzor — the RD parser fails and falls back to the legacy parser (often then ...","message":"The RD parser (`warning: RD parser failed ... falling back to legacy parser`) has gaps. When it fails AND the legacy fallback also fails, you get cryptic downstream errors (e.g. \"No main function found\"). User directive (2026-06-21): note EVERY RD parser issue here as a follow-up — important.\n\n\n`var dsc:Array\u003cFloat\u003e=[];` → `RD parser failed ... at offset N: expected type, found '\u003e='`. The lexer greedily tokenizes `\u003e=` as a single greater-equal token, swallowing the generic's closing `\u003e`, so the parser is mid-type when it hits `\u003e=`. Same token-splitting problem as `\u003e\u003e`/`\u003e\u003e=` in nested generics (`Array\u003cArray\u003cInt\u003e\u003e`). The RD parser must split a `\u003e=`/`\u003e\u003e`/`\u003e\u003e=` token back into `\u003e` + rest when closing a type-argument list (Haxe's parser does this re-lex).\n- Repro: any `:Array\u003cT\u003e=expr` with no space before `=`.\n- Workaround: space it — `var dsc:Array\u003cFloat\u003e = [];`.\n- Impact: falls back to legacy parser, which here ALSO failed → \"No main function found\" (silent: the real cause is the parse failure). Found while adding the pre-decode path to /tmp/q4chk/bench.hx ([[perf_haxe_qmatmul_blocked_on_simd_inlining]]).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_rd_parser_issues` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}